OneiOS take19, userland/metal/patches/NativeMetal/0015: the device answers the private queries every Metal process logged it
had no answer for.
Upstream: none (OneiOS).
What: bridge/NativeMetalObjects.rs device_answer answers five of Apple's private device queries itself where the runtime gives
no answer of the device's (metal2vulkan's PrivateAnswer::Metal: no document says what they promise), each held to what the
runtime does here: maxVertexAmplificationCount 2, Metal's measured value and one multiview pass on Turnip
(maxMultiviewViewCount 6; 1 without multiview); maxComputeThreadgroupMemory the public maxThreadgroupMemoryLength (Turnip's
maxComputeSharedMemorySize, 32 KiB on the Adreno 830; Metal's 64 KiB is twice the public length on Apple's GPUs, and a kernel
sized by it would be refused here); isLargeMRTSupported YES where the pass takes more than Apple1's 4 color attachments
(maxColorAttachments 8 here); supportsComputeCompressedTextureWrite NO (the runtime makes no Apple-compressed texture; an M3
Max answers NO too); supportsConditionalLoadStore NO (no document or AIR form names it: no client path is taken on a guess).
The frontend's installAnswers no longer asks the runtime for a query its device class answers itself (gpuCoreCount:
NBDeviceMethods' 8, which class_addMethod would not have replaced anyway).
Why: take18's iOS log, every process with a Metal device: "native-metal: no device answer: the device has no answer for
gpuCoreCount", and the same for maxVertexAmplificationCount, maxComputeThreadgroupMemory, isLargeMRTSupported,
supportsComputeCompressedTextureWrite and supportsConditionalLoadStore (22 processes x 6 lines in take17's). Unanswered, the
five fell to iOS 27's _MTLDevice (24A446's Metal): -[_MTLDevice isLargeMRTSupported] is `mov w0, #0`;
maxComputeThreadgroupMemory and maxVertexAmplificationCount read [self limits] +0x64 and +0xe4, which the frontend's empty
initLimits leaves zero; the two flags send familySupports... to the GPU family object at +0x270, which its empty
initGPUFamilySupport leaves nil. So 0 and NO, against the device's own public answers (maxThreadgroupMemoryLength 32768,
supportsVertexAmplificationCount: 2 YES, maxColorAttachments 8). Which clients ask is not known from here (the base class
answered them, so no "missing" line names them).
Switch: boot argument oneios-device-answers=0 (ONEIOS_DEVICE_ANSWERS=0): the five unanswered again (the base class's 0 and
NO). On by default: every answer is one the runtime executes, two keep the NO clients saw, and the changed three make the
device's answers agree with each other. Removable: yes (ONEIOS_METAL_SKIP=private-device-answers).
iOS's log: no "no device answer" line (with oneios-device-answers=0 the five come back, gpuCoreCount's does not).
